          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is coronary artery disease and how does it develop over time?",
          "answer": "Coronary artery disease (CAD) is the build-up of atherosclerotic plaque in the arteries that supply the heart muscle. The plaque forms over decades, starting with injury to the inner lining of the artery and a slow build-up of cholesterol-rich material beneath it. (Source: *Coronary artery disease*, general clinical reference.)\n\nThe condition can cause problems in two main ways:\n\n- **Gradual narrowing.** As the artery narrows, it can supply less blood when the heart works harder, often during exertion. This produces predictable chest discomfort, known as stable angina.\n- **Plaque rupture.** A plaque can crack, which exposes material that triggers a blood clot. The clot can block the artery suddenly. This is an acute coronary syndrome, including heart attack. It can happen at a plaque that never narrowed enough to cause symptoms, so a normal stress test in the past does not rule out a heart attack now. (Source: *Coronary artery disease*.)\n\nThe sources I searched do not describe CAD as a set of numbered stages, so I cannot give you a stage-by-stage timeline.\n\nSeveral factors are linked to developing CAD. The guidance on lipid screening lists age, sex, total and HDL cholesterol, blood pressure, diabetes, and smoking status as inputs to estimated heart and blood vessel risk. It also names family history of early cardiovascular disease, chronic kidney disease, and persistently high triglycerides as factors that can raise that risk. (Source: *Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation*, cardiology guidance.) Separately, sustained high blood pressure damages arteries over years, which is one reason it is treated even when it causes no symptoms. (Source: *Hypertension*, general clinical reference.)\n\n**Seek immediate emergency care** if you or someone with you has chest pain or pressure that is severe, does not settle, or comes with breathlessness, sweating, or nausea. Call your local emergency number rather than driving yourself. (Source: *Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack*, emergency guidance.)\n\nIf you have questions about your own heart health or risk, a clinician can assess your personal situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
